WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.505
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.505 --> 00:00:08.680
All right, I'm Glenn Carroll.

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:10.236
I teach a class at the Stanford

00:00:10.236 --> 00:00:12.106
Graduate School of Business titled

00:00:12.106 --> 00:00:13.546
Leading Through Culture.

00:00:13.546 --> 00:00:16.435
I'd like to talk to you about five

00:00:16.435 --> 00:00:19.227
key takeaways from this class.

00:00:19.227 --> 00:00:22.409
[MUSIC]

00:00:22.409 --> 00:00:24.253
In traditional management,

00:00:24.253 --> 00:00:26.393
the manager observes the worker,

00:00:26.393 --> 00:00:27.795
measures his output,

00:00:27.795 --> 00:00:29.935
coaches him gives him feedback and

00:00:29.935 --> 00:00:31.792
tells him how to do it better.

00:00:31.792 --> 00:00:33.412
In Cultural Management,

00:00:33.412 --> 00:00:34.811
you build the system,

00:00:34.811 --> 00:00:36.431
you trust the system, and

00:00:36.431 --> 00:00:38.353
you sit back and let it happen.

00:00:38.353 --> 00:00:40.458
The hardest times for a manager in

00:00:40.458 --> 00:00:42.413
a culture a managed system is in

00:00:42.413 --> 00:00:44.443
times of crisis because in times

00:00:44.443 --> 00:00:46.697
of crisis, the impulse is always to

00:00:46.697 --> 00:00:48.424
grab the wheel and to try and

00:00:48.424 --> 00:00:50.477
direct things to get in charge.

00:00:50.477 --> 00:00:52.187
But in a strong culture system, you

00:00:52.187 --> 00:00:53.783
just have to trust that the system

00:00:53.783 --> 00:00:55.208
has been built properly, and

00:00:55.208 --> 00:00:56.757
that things are going to work out.

00:00:56.757 --> 00:01:01.880
[MUSIC]

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:03.944
In his book no rules, rules.

00:01:03.944 --> 00:01:06.015
The CEO of Netflix Reed Hastings

00:01:06.015 --> 00:01:07.626
tells a story about how in

00:01:07.626 --> 00:01:09.467
Sheryl Sandberg, the CEO of

00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:11.846
Facebook, following him around all

00:01:11.846 --> 00:01:14.290
day to see how he managed.

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:15.998
At the end of the day he caught her

00:01:15.998 --> 00:01:17.706
say, the amazing thing was this

00:01:17.706 --> 00:01:19.414
with you all day long, and to see

00:01:19.414 --> 00:01:21.390
that you didn't make one decision.

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:24.725
Reed smiled and said I felt great,

00:01:24.725 --> 00:01:27.050
because that's exactly what we were

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:27.885
aiming for.

00:01:27.885 --> 00:01:29.355
The reason he could do this is

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:30.828
because Netflix has a strong

00:01:30.828 --> 00:01:32.730
culture, and the culture has people

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:34.264
who are making decisions very

00:01:34.264 --> 00:01:35.797
similarly to the way that Reed

00:01:35.797 --> 00:01:37.575
would have made them himself, but

00:01:37.575 --> 00:01:38.769
he didn't have to make

00:01:38.769 --> 00:01:43.053
[MUSIC]

00:01:43.053 --> 00:01:45.669
The third key takeaway is that you

00:01:45.669 --> 00:01:48.286
can build a strong culture around

00:01:48.286 --> 00:01:50.810
any set of beliefs, values, or

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:51.851
practices.

00:01:51.851 --> 00:01:53.334
This is what organisational

00:01:53.334 --> 00:01:55.251
theorists call cultural content and

00:01:55.251 --> 00:01:57.105
cultural content Is irrelevant for

00:01:57.105 --> 00:01:58.525
whether you have a strong or

00:01:58.525 --> 00:01:59.960
weak culture.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:01.090
Don't believe it?

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:03.265
Consider the fact that I could name

00:02:03.265 --> 00:02:05.065
examples of strong cultural

00:02:05.065 --> 00:02:07.240
organizations from businesses of

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:09.715
all kinds, manufacturing, retail,

00:02:09.715 --> 00:02:12.380
service, finance, you name it.

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:15.085
Religious cults, schools, terrorist

00:02:15.085 --> 00:02:17.472
cells, urban gangs, SWAT teams,

00:02:17.472 --> 00:02:19.539
military special forces, the

00:02:19.539 --> 00:02:21.846
content is irrelevant for whether

00:02:21.846 --> 00:02:23.925
the culture is strong or weak.

00:02:23.925 --> 00:02:29.020
[MUSIC]

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:31.283
Most managers use three key levers,

00:02:31.283 --> 00:02:32.743
defining the culture,

00:02:32.743 --> 00:02:35.008
setting up incentives and launching

00:02:35.008 --> 00:02:37.940
training communication program.

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:39.388
But in fact there are something

00:02:39.388 --> 00:02:40.893
like 20 different levers that

00:02:40.893 --> 00:02:43.000
are available for a manager to use.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:44.863
They include things like making

00:02:44.863 --> 00:02:46.797
the organization transparent,

00:02:46.797 --> 00:02:48.592
homogenizing the groupings,

00:02:48.592 --> 00:02:50.595
broadening the job definitions,

00:02:50.595 --> 00:02:52.700
instituting job rotation.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:54.726
This list of levers is long and

00:02:54.726 --> 00:02:57.157
can be used in many different ways,

00:02:57.157 --> 00:02:59.021
it's a mistake not to use or

00:02:59.021 --> 00:03:01.209
considered these the full set of

00:03:01.209 --> 00:03:01.872
levers.

00:03:01.872 --> 00:03:07.397
[MUSIC]

00:03:07.397 --> 00:03:09.153
So the fifth key takeaway is about

00:03:09.153 --> 00:03:10.785
the most impactful managerial

00:03:10.785 --> 00:03:12.667
lever, it is culturally selective

00:03:12.667 --> 00:03:14.483
hiring, you can't always do it,

00:03:14.483 --> 00:03:15.673
you need to be hiring,

00:03:15.673 --> 00:03:17.750
you probably need to be growing.

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:19.091
To do it requires lots of time and

00:03:19.091 --> 00:03:19.693
interviews.

00:03:19.693 --> 00:03:21.295
Cultural destructive hiring is

00:03:21.295 --> 00:03:22.480
often criticized today

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:24.138
because if it's not done right,

00:03:24.138 --> 00:03:25.738
it can introduce a lot of bias and

00:03:25.738 --> 00:03:27.230
discrimination.

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:29.213
Because when you select on cultural

00:03:29.213 --> 00:03:31.128
traits, you also get a lot of other

00:03:31.128 --> 00:03:32.978
things that are more homogeneous

00:03:32.978 --> 00:03:34.810
than you might like.

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:35.932
The issue though,

00:03:35.932 --> 00:03:38.044
can be resolved and done properly.

00:03:38.044 --> 00:03:42.437
And the important thing to remember

00:03:42.437 --> 00:03:46.831
is the culturally selective hiring

00:03:46.831 --> 00:03:50.931
works and it is the most impactful

00:03:50.931 --> 00:03:54.738
thing to build a strong culture

00:03:54.738 --> 00:03:58.122
quickly and effectively.

